<body bgcolor=white background="Edward_Y_Chang_files/image004.gif"
lang=EN-US link="#FF3C00" vlink="#DF9300">

<div class=Section1>

<h1><span style='font-size:14.0pt;font-family:"Palatino Linotype";color:black'>NSF
IIS 0535085 Project Home Page</span></h1>

<h1><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Palatino Linotype";color:black'>Scalable,
Multimodal Algorithms for Multimedia Information Retrieval</span></h1>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:10.0pt;font-family:"Palatino Linotype";
color:black'><a href="http://infolab.stanford.edu/~echang/">Edward Y. Chang</a>,
 University of California, Santa Barbara</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:10.0pt;font-family:"Palatino Linotype"'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:.5in;text-indent:-.5in'><span
style='font-family:"Palatino Linotype";color:blue'>I.<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span><span style='font-family:"Palatino Linotype";color:blue'>Project
Summary</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:.25in'><span style='font-size:10.0pt;
font-family:"Palatino Linotype";color:black'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:10.0pt;font-family:"Palatino Linotype";
color:black'>The principal design goal of a multimedia retrieval system is to
return data images or video clips that accurately match users' <i>query
concepts</i> (e.g., `animals,' `landscape' or `architectures'). To achieve this
design goal, the system must first comprehend a user's query concept
thoroughly, and then search efficiently through large repositories to find
images or videos that match the concept. These concept-learning and
efficient-retrieval requirements pose new scalability challenges for the
learning algorithms and indexing methods that do the job. Three major
challenges are 1) <i>scarcity of training data</i>, 2) <i>scalability of
learning algorithms</i>, and 3) <i>non-traditional and high-dimensional queries</i>.
This proposed project will address these challenges in three separate but
interrelated research thrusts.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><i><span style='font-size:10.0pt;font-family:"Palatino Linotype";
color:black'>&nbsp;</span></i></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:.25in;text-indent:-.25in'><span
style='font-size:10.0pt;font-family:Wingdings;color:black'><img width=4
height=11 src="Edward_Y_Chang_files/image001.gif" alt="*"><span
style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span><i><span style='font-size:10.0pt;font-family:"Palatino Linotype";
color:black'>Multimodal active learning algorithms.</span></i><span
style='font-size:10.0pt;font-family:"Palatino Linotype";color:black'> Keywords
and perceptual features will be jointly explored for devising <i>active-learning
</i>algorithms that can learn a target query-concept based on the concept's
characteristics.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:10.0pt;font-family:"Palatino Linotype";
color:black'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:.25in;text-indent:-.25in'><span
style='font-size:10.0pt;font-family:Wingdings;color:black'><img width=4
height=11 src="Edward_Y_Chang_files/image001.gif" alt="*"><span
style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span><i><span style='font-size:10.0pt;font-family:"Palatino Linotype";
color:black'>Scalable kernel machines</span></i><span style='font-size:10.0pt;
font-family:"Palatino Linotype";color:black'>. Kernel machines such as kernel
PCA and Support Vector Machines (SVMs) are essential statistical tools for
analyzing and annotating media data, but their computational intensity is O(n<sup>3</sup>).
This research thrust will investigate <i>recursive</i> <i>decomposition</i> and

<i>parallel</i> algorithms to reduce the computational intensity to O(log<sup>2</sup>
n).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:10.0pt;font-family:"Palatino Linotype";
color:black'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:.25in;text-indent:-.25in'><span
style='font-size:10.0pt;font-family:Wingdings;color:black'><img width=4
height=11 src="Edward_Y_Chang_files/image001.gif" alt="*"><span
style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span><i><span style='font-size:10.0pt;font-family:"Palatino Linotype";
color:black'>Kernel indexers for hyperplane queries</span></i><span
style='font-size:10.0pt;font-family:"Palatino Linotype";color:black'>.&nbsp; A
concept learned by SVMs is a hyperplane, not a point. This research thrust will
develop novel high-dimensional indexing methods to support hyperplane queries
to work with kernel methods in general, and SVMs in particular.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:10.0pt;font-family:"Palatino Linotype";
color:black'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:10.0pt;font-family:"Palatino Linotype";
color:black'>The <i>scalable kernel machines</i> thrust improves the efficiency
to analyze and annotate multimedia data. The <i>multimodal active learning</i>
thrust uses the analysis and annotation to perform concept-dependent
learning.&nbsp; The <i>kernel indexer</i> thrust makes the other two thrusts
efficient for retrieval of data matching certain criteria. Together, these
three integrated research thrusts provide a solid foundation for building
large-scale, next-generation, multimedia information-retrieval systems.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:10.0pt;font-family:"Palatino Linotype";
color:black'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:.5in;text-indent:-.5in'><span
style='font-family:"Palatino Linotype";color:blue'>II.<span style='font:7.0pt "Times New Roman"'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span><span style='font-family:"Palatino Linotype";color:blue'>Participants
&amp; Collaborators</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-family:"Palatino Linotype";color:blue'>&nbsp;</span></p>

